        "text": "In proof of work hashing is used as we've discussed before, what we didn't mention is that there's a specific hash that nodes need to find. They hash the contents of the block they want to broadcast to the network and hope the hash matches the parameters set out by the blockchain. Remember that our block must also include the hash of the previous block so it can only be created after the most recent one. In proof of work systems it's often incredibly difficult to calculate a valid hash. Nodes use large amounts of computational power and electricity to do this. But here's the thing. Once they do find one, they send the block around the network. Anyone can easily check that it's valid and hasn't been edited because they only need to hash it once to see whether it produces a correct hash. They'll check all of the information inside the block too, whether it was built on a previous block and whether the transactions are authentic.",
        "start": 400,
